{"type":"text","timestamp":1794872803294,"sessionID":"ses_e193f3330ffeih7BC9i0XRd5Kp","part":{"id":"prt_1e6c16568001hLq0tOB9bGELQm","messageID":"msg_1e6c14117001YfTXuRD6Ja0eyO","sessionID":"ses_e193f3330ffeih7BC9i0XRd5Kp","type":"text","text":"The test shows the logic is working - the sigstore verification fails (missing library), so the range isn't added to `ranges`, and thus the `.ots` check doesn't run. This is correct behavior. Let me verify with a more complete test by mocking the sigstore verification.\n","time":{"start":1794872796520,"end":1794872803292}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794872803391,"sessionID":"ses_e193f3330ffeih7BC9i0XRd5Kp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call-7978a54e-935a-4d19-8607-57cc5e24f9fb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && python3 -c \"\nimport tempfile, os, json, hashlib, subprocess, shutil, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, 'record')\nfrom record import canonical, load_chain, range_predicate\n\n# Create a test ledger\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'inbox'))\n\n# Create two entries\nchain = []\nfor i in range(2):\n    e = {'seq': i+1, 'prev': chain[-1][1] if chain else None, 'kind': 'plan',\n         'written_at': '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z',\n         'body': {'by': 'proposer-agent', 'claim': 'test', 'test': 'test', 'decision_rule': 'test'}}\n    raw = canonical(e)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', f'{i+1:06d}.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw)\n    chain.append((e, hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()))\n\n# Create a sigstore.json that mimics a valid attestation for range 1-2\n# We need to create a bundle that will pass check_sigstore\n# Since we can't easily do that without sigstore, let's directly test the check_anchors logic\n# by monkey-patching check_sigstore\n\nimport record\noriginal_check_sigstore = record.check_sigstore\n\ndef mock_check_sigstore(bundle, digest, repo, workflow, ref):\n    # Return a valid predicate for range 1-2\n    predicate = range_predicate(chain, 1, 2)\n    return 1234567890, predicate, 'abcdef123456'\n\nrecord.check_sigstore = mock_check_sigstore\n\ntry:\n    # Run check-anchors - should fail because 000002.ots is missing\n    result = subprocess.run(['python3', 'record/record.py', '--ledger', d, 'check-anchors'], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    print('Exit code:', result.returncode)\n    print('stderr:', result.stderr)\n    print('stdout:', result.stdout)\nfinally:\n    record.check_sigstore = original_check_sigstore\n\nshutil.rmtree(d)\n\""},"output":"Exit code: 1\nstderr: BAD: entries 1-2 were never attested by the writer\n\nstdout: {\n \"anchors\": [],\n \"entries\": 2,\n \"writer_attested_through\": 0,\n \"writer_runs\": [],\n \"wrong\": [\n  \"entries 1-2 were never attested by the writer\"\n ],\n \"could_not_check\": []\n}\n\n","metadata":{"output":"Exit code: 1\nstderr: BAD: entries 1-2 were never attested by the writer\n\nstdout: {\n \"anchors\": [],\n \"entries\": 2,\n \"writer_attested_through\": 0,\n \"writer_runs\": [],\n \"wrong\": [\n  \"entries 1-2 were never attested by the writer\"\n ],\n \"could_not_check\": []\n}\n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && python3 -c \"\nimport tempfile, os, json, hashlib, subprocess, shutil, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, 'record')\nfrom record import canonical, load_chain, range_predicate\n\n# Create a test ledger\nd = tempfile.mkdtemp()\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'entries'))\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'anchors'))\nos.makedirs(os.path.join(d, 'inbox'))\n\n# Create two entries\nchain = []\nfor i in range(2):\n    e = {'seq': i+1, 'prev': chain[-1][1] if chain else None, 'kind': 'plan',\n         'written_at': '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z',\n         'body': {'by': 'proposer-agent', 'claim': 'test', 'test': 'test', 'decision_rule': 'test'}}\n    raw = canonical(e)\n    with open(os.path.join(d, 'entries', f'{i+1:06d}.json'), 'wb') as f:\n        f.write(raw)\n    chain.append((e, hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()))\n\n# Create a sigstore.json that mimics a valid attestation for range 1-2\n# We need to create a bundle that will pass check_sigstore\n# Since we can't easily do that without sigstore, let's directly test the check_anchors logic\n# by monkey-patching check_sigstore\n\nimport record\noriginal_check_sigstore = record.check_sigstore\n\ndef mock_check_sigstore(bundle, digest, repo, workflow, ref):\n    # Return a valid predicate for range 1-2\n    predicate = range_predicate(chain, 1, 2)\n    return 1234567890, predicate, 'abcdef123456'\n\nrecord.check_sigstore = mock_check_sigstore\n\ntry:\n    # Run check-anchors - should fail because 000002.ots is missing\n    result = subprocess.run(['python3', 'record/record.py', '--ledger', d, 'check-anchors'], capture_output=True, text=True)\n    print('Exit code:', result.returncode)\n    print('stderr:', result.stderr)\n    print('stdout:', result.stdout)\nfinally:\n    record.check_sigstore = original_check_sigstore\n\nshutil.rmtree(d)\n\"","time":{"start":1794872803288,"end":1794872803382}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The test shows that the sigstore verification fails because the module isn't installed, so the range attestation isn't verified. Let me create a better test that mocks or creates a valid sigstore bundle, but that's complex. Instead, let me check if my code is even being reached. The issue is that the sigstore verification fails with CantCheck, so the range is never added to `ranges`, and thus the check for missing .ots files never runs.\n\nLet me create a simpler test - I'll check if the .ots missing check is in the right place. Looking at the code flow:\n1. The sigstore anchor is processed\n2. If it passes verification, it's added to `ranges`\n3. After the loop, we iterate over `ranges` and check for missing .ots\n\nSince the sigstore verification fails (no sigstore module), the range is never added to `ranges`. This is actually correct behavior - if we can't verify the sigstore attestation, we can't trust it to know which range heads need .ots proofs.\n\nLet me test with a valid (mocked) sigstore bundle that passes verification. Actually, let me just check if the logic is correct by looking at the code path. The check for missing .ots is inside the loop over `sorted(ranges)`, which only includes successfully verified sigstore anchors.\n\nLet me create a better test that uses a mocked check_sigstore or a simpler approach. Actually, let me just verify the logic by creating a test that directly tests the check-anchors function with a mock.\n\nBut wait - the issue is that the existing test `test_check_anchors_requires_the_writer_to_cover_every_entry` passes. That test creates a ledger with 2 entries and runs check-anchors without any anchors, expecting it to fail with \"never attested by the writer\". That still passes.\n\nLet me think about how to properly test the new behavior. Since we can't easily create valid sigstore bundles in a test without the sigstore library, let me trace through the logic manually:\n\n1. If a valid sigstore.json exists for entry N attesting range X-N, it gets added to `ranges` as (X, N, commit)\n2. Then in the loop over `sorted(ranges)`, for each (lo, hi, commit), we check if `{hi:06d}.ots` exists\n3. If not, we add `bad.append(f\"{hi:06d}.ots is missing\")`\n\nThis logic looks correct. The check is performed for each range head (hi) that was successfully verified via sigstore.\n\nLet me verify the test case in the bug report: \"anchors/000002.ots is missing\" where \"anchors/000002.sigstore.json attests the range ending at entry 2\". So the sigstore file is for entry 2, and it attests a range ending at entry 2. The missing .ots file would be 000002.ots.\n\nMy code checks for `{hi:06d}.ots` where hi is the `to_seq` from the predicate, which matches the sigstore file's sequence number. This is correct.\n\nLet me also check if there's an edge case: what if there are multiple sigstore anchors for the same range head? The code would check the .ots file multiple times, but that's fine - it would just add the same error multiple times.\n\nActually, wait - I should also check if the .ots file check should be done for ALL sigstore anchors, or only for the range heads. The bug report says \"the .ots proof for any attested range head is missing\". The range head is the `to_seq` of each range. My code checks exactly that.\n\nLet me run the full test suite again to make sure everything still works.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e6c17fd3001XJf7DAGDj5xzBK","sessionID":"ses_e193f3330ffeih7BC9i0XRd5Kp","messageID":"msg_1e6c14117001YfTXuRD6Ja0eyO"}}
